# A complete example

From the sibling `zast` library directory, save this as `example_test.zig`.
The test uses `std.testing.allocator` so allocation leaks are reported.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const zast = @import("zast");

test "render an inferred enum" {
    const allocator = std.testing.allocator;
    var context: zast.AstContext = .{};
    defer context.deinit(allocator);
    _ = try context.addNode(allocator, .root, 0, undefined);

    const status: zast.EnumDeclaration = .{
        .backing_type = null,
        .fields = &.{
            .{ .name = "unknown" },
            .{ .name = "ok" },
        },
    };
    try status.emit(allocator, &context, "Status", .public);

    var tree = try context.finish(allocator);
    const owned_source = tree.source;
    defer allocator.free(owned_source);
    defer tree.deinit(allocator);

    const rendered_source = try tree.renderAlloc(allocator);
    defer allocator.free(rendered_source);
    try std.testing.expectEqualStrings(
        "pub const Status = enum {\n" ++
            "    unknown,\n" ++
            "    ok,\n" ++
            "};\n",
        rendered_source,
    );
}
```

Run it from the `zast` library directory:

```console
$ zig test --dep zast -Mroot=example_test.zig -Mzast=src/root.zig
```

The value of `rendered_source` is:

```zig
pub const Status = enum {
    unknown,
    ok,
};
```

# Follow the lifecycle

1. Create an empty unmanaged `zast.AstContext` and reserve node zero as `.root`.
   Its placeholder data is filled by `finish()`.
2. Describe an enum. This stack value borrows its field slice and names; no
   descriptor allocation is needed for this fixed example.
3. Call `emit()` with the allocator and context. It emits the declaration's
   token spellings and node links, then registers the declaration at the root.
4. Call `finish()` once after emission. The resulting tree owns its token,
   node, and extra-data buffers. Its source allocation is released separately.
5. Call `renderAlloc()` to allocate the formatted Zig string.
6. Release the formatted string, AST buffers, saved source slice, and remaining
   context buffers. Zig executes the `defer` statements in reverse order.

Use the same allocator for all operations on a context and its finished tree.
Descriptions can use another arena, but every borrowed name, field slice, and
recursive expression must stay valid until emission finishes. After an emission
or finalization allocation failure, clean up the partial context; do not render
or retry it as a complete AST.

# Change one property

A `null` backing type generates `enum { ... }`. Set `.backing_type = "u8"` to
emit `enum(u8) { ... }`. Explicit field values and a non-exhaustive catch-all
require an explicit backing type. Use the [online examples](/examples/) to inspect
those forms without setting up another native program.

